details.image_prompt:
[
  {
    "prompt": "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The composition features two simple, round, matte ceramic plates positioned on a worn, rustic aged oak kitchen table. The main plate is centered and sharp in the foreground, with the second plate slightly blurred in the background. The camera angle is a 3/4 overhead view (40°) to capture the volume and texture of the slaw. Soft natural side light from a window on the left creates gentle highlights on the dressing-coated vegetables and subtle, natural shadows. The slaw consists of finely shaved, bright green Brussels sprouts, visibly massaged and tenderized, tossed with glistening, juicy orange segments that provide bright pops of amber-orange color. The surface has a realistic sheen from the olive oil and Dijon vinaigrette. Coarsely chopped green and purple-hued pistachios are sprinkled unevenly over the top, adding crunchy texture. A rumpled, neutral-toned linen napkin is tucked partially under the plate, and a few stray, bright green Brussels sprout leaves and a single piece of orange segment lie naturally on the wooden surface near the plate, suggesting a lived-in preparation. No sterile or artificial elements. Includes slight imperfections like a tiny, realistic sauce drip on the rim of the plate and a scattering of finely ground black pepper on the wood. The color temperature is warm and inviting, consistent with golden-hour kitchen light. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene captures an authentic, home-cooked moment. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. Indistinguishable from professional editorial food photography for a high-end cookbook."
  }
]
